  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 220 AM EDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\n===== Friday into the Weekend =====\n\nThe upper trough will remain over the eastern US on Friday, but is \nexpected to shift east by Saturday night. The upper pattern becomes a \nbit more flattened by Sunday, but will begin to see low-amplitude \nridging setting up over the Upper Midwest. Dry and sunny weather is \nexpected for Friday and the weekend as sfc high pressure builds \nacross the Ohio Valley. Temperatures range from the mid to upper 80s \non Friday, but could warm slightly into the lower 90s by Sunday. \nDespite this slight uptick in temps, dewpoints look to remain on the \nmore comfortable side and in the 60s each day. \n\n===== Monday through Wednesday =====\n\nWe'll continue the warming and dry trend into the first half of next \nweek, with the upper pattern featuring flattened ridging as troughs \npass by across southern Canada. Confidence continues to grow on \nseeing above normal temps next week, but the NBM bias continues to \noutput forecast highs up into the mid 90s next week. The 100-member \nLREF is not as impressed with temps next week, giving only a 20-30% \nchance for temps to exceed 90F each afternoon. We'll likely end up \nseeing most areas hit the upper 80s and low 90s next week, with \nperhaps our urban islands creeping a few degrees warmer into the mid \n90s next week. Heat indices will mostly hover around the upper 90s \nnext week as dewpoints sit in the upper 60s, but we'll begin to see \na slightly better moisture fetch by Wednesday to creep dewpoints \ninto the low to mid 70s, leading to some slightly elevated heat \nindices. \n\n\nExtended Forecast Discussion...\n\nLooking beyond Wednesday, the long term ensembles and AI Ensemble \nguidance suggest the upper level pattern will undergo some changes. \nThe upper level ridge that is expected to bring dry/warm weather for \nmuch of the week is expected to break down as a series of mid-level \nwaves push across the CONUS.  While the details are not overly \nclear, it does look like that we'll have some passing upper troughs \nthat will drive some cold frontal boundaries through the region. \nWhile much of next week will feature above normal temperatures and \nbelow normal precipitation, we expect temps to come down a bit to \nnear normal with precipitation going up to normal or slightly above \nnormal.",
